{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 film directed by david fincher that tells the story of the founding of facebook, the social media giant that has revolutionized the way people interact with each other. the movie is based on the book \"the accidental billionaires\" by ben mezrich, which chronicles the rise of mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and ceo of facebook, from his harvard days to the company's early success.\n\nthe film begins with mark zuckerberg (played by jesse eisenberg), a sophomore at harvard, being dumped by his girlfriend, erica albright (played by railey mcconnell). in a fit of anger and hurt, mark creates a website called \"facemash\" that allows users to compare the photos of two harvard students and vote on which one is more attractive. the site becomes an instant hit, but also generates a lot of controversy and gets mark into trouble with the harvard administration.\n\nlater, mark is approached by twin brothers cameron and tyler winklevoss (played by armie hammer), who are members of the harvard rowing team and are looking to create a social networking site for harvard students. mark agrees to help them, but eventually decides to create his own site, which he calls \"thefacebook\". with the help of his friend eduardo savernin (played by andrew garfield), mark launches the site, which quickly gains popularity among harvard students.\n\nas facebook grows in popularity, mark and eduardo become embroiled in a dispute over the direction of the company and the ownership of its shares. meanwhile, the winklevoss twins claim that mark stole their idea and sue him for intellectual property theft.\n\nthe film is notable for its nuanced portrayal of mark zuckerberg, who is depicted as a complex and multifaceted character. jesse eisenberg's performance is outstanding, capturing mark's intelligence, ambition, and social awkwardness. the supporting cast is also excellent, with andrew garfield bringing a lot of depth and nuance to the role of eduardo savernin.\n\nthe film's cinematography is also noteworthy, with a muted color palette and a lot of close-ups that create a sense of intimacy and immediacy. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is also excellent, adding to the film's tense and unsettling atmosphere.\n\none of the strengths of the film is its exploration of the themes of ambition, friendship, and betrayal. the movie raises important questions about the ethics of social media and the consequences of our actions online. it also provides a fascinating insight into the world of high-tech entrepreneurship and the cutthroat nature of the startup ecosystem.\n\noverall, the social network is a gripping and thought-provoking film that is well worth watching. it's a great example of how a well-made movie can spark important conversations and debates about the issues of our time. if you're interested in technology, entrepreneurship, or just great storytelling, then this is a film that you won't want to miss.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -1.2056829929351807}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0003196682082489133}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.01104797888547182}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.9149515628814697}, {"token": "201", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0005799042410217226}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.00033539868309162557}, {"token": " by", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " david", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.10195178538560867}, {"token": " tells", "logprob": -0.0012000747956335545}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " story", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " the"